  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n\nThis plan adopts a hybrid evolutionary-gradient approach to overcome the limitations of the previous optimization strategy. The core idea is to:\n\n- Use a parallel multi-start strategy based on the Frank-Wolfe algorithm with adaptive simplex projection to explore a broader solution space\n- Combine this with a sophisticated surrogate modeling technique using adaptive K-Friedman regression to approximate the expensive-to-compute overlap integral\n- Implement a resolution-sweeping mechanism that dynamically adjusts discretization based on error analysis\n- Enhance population diversity through domain-adaptive crossover operators that respect the underlying problem structure\n\nThe method is designed to escape local optima by:\n- Exploiting gradient information through the Frank-Wolfe framework\n- Maintaining mathematical integrity through projection-based constraint handling\n- Leveraging evolutionary pressure through a multi-start procedure\n- Using a warm-start strategy that intelligently transfers solutions between resolutions\n\nKey differences from the previous approach include:\n1. The introduction of an adaptive simplex projection that better preserves mathematical structure\n2. The implementation of domain-adaptive crossover that considers discrete event simulation analogies\n3. Dynamic error estimation through adaptive surrogate modeling\n4. A resolution-sweeping mechanism that intelligently chooses between maintaining parent resolution and exploring densification\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n\nVariables:\n- h_weighted: The main solution vector of dimension n\n- z: A simplex projection vector with same dimension as h_weighted\n- f_surrogate: The surrogate approximation function with smoothing parameter \u03b2\n- \u03b5: The discretization error estimate\n- \u03b7: The regularization parameter for projection\n\nThe objective function is approximated by a differentiable surrogate:\n\nmin_z max_\u03c4 (1/\u03b2) * log(1 + \u2211exp(-\u03b2 * [\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+\u03c4))dx]))\n\nWhere the max operation is approximated using exponential weighting.\n\nConstraints are handled through:\n- A projection function P(h) = clamp(h + s, 0, 1) where s adjusts the sum\n- Domain adaptation term in crossover operations\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\nStep 1: Input handling and initial setup\n- Accept seed, budget, and optional initial_h_values\n- Set base resolution n_init = 300 (if parent not available, use 500)\n- Initialize best values with infinity\n\nStep 2: Multi-start framework\n- Generate M = 5 populations at distinct resolutions (starting from n_init and doubling)\n- Initialize using either initial_h_values or uniform random distribution\n\nStep 3: Adaptive Frank-Wolfe iteration\n- For each population:\n  - Perform line search: s = argmax_z' \u27e8\u2207f_surrogate(z), z'\u27e9 s.t. ||z'||\u221e\u22641\n  - Update using adaptive step size\n  - Clip to domain and adjust sum\n  - Apply domain-adaptive crossover with probability p_cross\n\nStep 4: Surrogate adaptation\n- Update surrogate every 100 iterations with decreasing \u03b2\n- Use K-Friedman regression to fit 10th degree polynomials to approximated lags\n- Estimate discretization error term using jackknife method\n\nStep 5: Termination condition\n- Stop when no improvement in 500 iterations\n- Or elapsed time > budget\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n\nBase configuration:\n- Population size: 25\n- Cross probability: 0.8\n- Mapping rate: 0.4\n- \u03b2 starts at 15 and decreases exponentially\n- Frank-Wolfe iterations per population: 1500\n\nKey variations to try:\n- Resolution doubling: Start at n_init and double until time allows\n- Warm start: Reuse best solution from lower resolutions to raise baseline\n- Smart projection: Use adaptive projection that limits change to recent entries\n\n5. Computation budget:\n\n- Baseline n=300 solution: ~20 seconds (1100/5)\n- GA/Frank-Wolfe: 900 seconds total time\n- Resolution doubling: If between 3-5\u00d7 performance improvement\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Time monitoring at start of each major phase\n- Gradient clipping to [-5,5]\n- Warm restart every 500 iterations\n- Progressive \u03b2 reduction prevents negative culling\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n\n- Verify: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and sum(h)=n/2\n- Re-evaluate candidate using exact c5 computation\n- Final score: c5 with at least 1e-8 precision\n- Main index: resolution n, array h, c5 value\n\nFallback strategy:\n- If algorithm fails, return parent solution with initial_h_values\n- Use if not_feasible filter with gradient-based recovery",
